A||||1||Hartz||Interior Cleaning Tip||||6||
Z||000000||Hartz||05-14-2001||11:42 AM||||OK, maybe I'm the last guy on earth to have thought of this, but in case I'm not - I thought I'd pass on a tip from this weekend's cleaning thrash on my new project car.<P>My interior was kind of grungy.  The previous owner was a heck of an engineer but not much of a detailer.  I started cleaning the light grey parts with Simple Green, but even that wasn't getting some of the grime off.  As a last ditch effort, I tried an SOS pad from the kitchen.  AMAZING!  17 years of gunk and dirt came off with just light scrubbing.  Even the nasty black marks on the side of the doors!  I followed it up with Simple Green and an old toothbrush, and a light coat of Armor All, and it looks NEW.  Even the wife was impressed.<P>I was worried that the SOS pad would really tear up the soft vinyl, but it didn't.  It had just enough abrasiveness to really clean without scratching.<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<P>||||reg||6
Z||000001||Fiero5||05-14-2001||12:14 PM||||Hey, that sounds too good to be true?<P>I will have to try that. Anything that can help speed up cleaning some areas would be helpfull, especially with the ever growing number of Fieros we have. Our only parts car was in such good shape (58k miles!), we have decided to fix it up and run it. It doesn't really need anything major, just a few panels and such. The interior was mint including both seats.<BR> &gt;sigh&lt; I guess I will never have a parts car. Oh well, with Bob Parker at Kick Hill Farms only a few hours away, who needs a parts car?<P>Anyway, thanks for the cool tip Hartz.<P>Steve<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero1-pic2.JPG">   <BR>http://www.kelleyfamilyfieros.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||10
